WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.220 --> 00:00:02.810
- It's the one and only Oprah Winfrey.

00:00:02.810 --> 00:00:05.614
(audience applauding)

00:00:05.614 --> 00:00:06.705
- Hey, hey hey.

00:00:06.705 --> 00:00:10.413
(audience applauding)

00:00:10.413 --> 00:00:13.163
(Drew screaming)

00:00:16.258 --> 00:00:20.230
- This is the loudest we've
been since we started in August,

00:00:20.230 --> 00:00:22.920
just us in a very big room,

00:00:22.920 --> 00:00:27.320
kind of starting in an unprecedented time.

00:00:27.320 --> 00:00:28.340
Welcome to the show.

00:00:28.340 --> 00:00:30.713
Dare I say, maybe you've seen it?

00:00:31.840 --> 00:00:32.920
- Yes, I have.

00:00:32.920 --> 00:00:37.920
May I say, dare I say, seeing
it actually changed my life.

00:00:39.230 --> 00:00:40.990
You have changed my life.

00:00:40.990 --> 00:00:43.450
You certainly changed my
work life because I was there

00:00:43.450 --> 00:00:47.680
for that very first show where you beamed

00:00:47.680 --> 00:00:51.080
in Cameron and there, you all were.

00:00:51.080 --> 00:00:53.510
And we were, you know, didn't know

00:00:53.510 --> 00:00:56.440
that you all weren't in the same space.

00:00:56.440 --> 00:00:58.967
I watched that and I said,

00:00:58.967 --> 00:01:01.638
"Hey, why can't we do what Drew did?"

00:01:01.638 --> 00:01:03.347
(Drew laughing)

00:01:03.347 --> 00:01:05.170
"Why can't we do what Drew did?"

00:01:05.170 --> 00:01:10.043
And since that time, for Oprah
conversations, for Apple TV,

00:01:11.240 --> 00:01:12.540
the first time I did it was

00:01:12.540 --> 00:01:16.950
with president Barack Obama, talking about

00:01:16.950 --> 00:01:18.510
his book 'Promised Land'.

00:01:18.510 --> 00:01:22.780
And there we were sitting
right across from each other

00:01:22.780 --> 00:01:25.730
in my living room, in
front of the fireplace.

00:01:25.730 --> 00:01:29.210
And I said, then, this
is because of you, Drew.

00:01:29.210 --> 00:01:30.393
So thank you.
(audience applauding)

00:01:30.393 --> 00:01:33.496
Pioneer woman.
(audience applauding)

00:01:33.496 --> 00:01:35.330
- I have to thank you so much

00:01:35.330 --> 00:01:39.240
because you didn't need to give
us the credit, but you did.

00:01:39.240 --> 00:01:43.110
And for those who are not aware,
here's a little taped piece

00:01:43.110 --> 00:01:47.423
on the technology of which
Oprah and I are talking about.

00:01:48.600 --> 00:01:51.600
When we launched this show
in the middle of a pandemic,

00:01:51.600 --> 00:01:53.230
we had to get creative.

00:01:53.230 --> 00:01:55.820
With the magic of technology,
we came up with a way

00:01:55.820 --> 00:01:57.490
to transport our guests

00:01:57.490 --> 00:02:00.710
from our LA studio
right here, to New York.

00:02:00.710 --> 00:02:01.970
How cool is that?

00:02:01.970 --> 00:02:04.650
We knew one person who thought so too.

00:02:04.650 --> 00:02:07.523
It's Oprah! It's Oprah! It's Oprah!

00:02:09.161 --> 00:02:11.494
Oprah approves! Oprah likey!

00:02:12.510 --> 00:02:15.320
Yes, the Oprah was inspired to use

00:02:15.320 --> 00:02:18.040
a similar green screen
technology to interview guests

00:02:18.040 --> 00:02:20.220
like President Barack Obama.

00:02:20.220 --> 00:02:23.390
Listen, we may not be able
to be in the same room,

00:02:23.390 --> 00:02:26.559
but we can sure feel
like it and look like it.

00:02:26.559 --> 00:02:27.992
(audience applauding)

00:02:27.992 --> 00:02:32.992
- I thank you so much because
I'm gonna be really honest,

00:02:33.980 --> 00:02:36.820
I haven't really admitted this out loud,

00:02:36.820 --> 00:02:41.540
but it was really scary
for me to be, you know,

00:02:41.540 --> 00:02:46.110
a girl, a woman, I don't
know what I am, a female,

00:02:46.110 --> 00:02:48.970
asking for people to take this risk

00:02:48.970 --> 00:02:52.980
and spend this money and ask
ourselves these questions.

00:02:52.980 --> 00:02:54.930
And I'm so grateful

00:02:54.930 --> 00:02:57.750
to my bosses that they
allowed us to do this,

00:02:57.750 --> 00:02:59.670
because this was, you know,

00:02:59.670 --> 00:03:03.430
a check and a risk away
from not happening.

00:03:03.430 --> 00:03:07.730
And I want to ask you, is
this where we're going?

00:03:07.730 --> 00:03:08.820
The water is out of the bottle.

00:03:08.820 --> 00:03:11.400
People are not gonna travel
the way they used to.

00:03:11.400 --> 00:03:14.740
People have gotten comfortable
with interview styles.

00:03:14.740 --> 00:03:16.520
Some are gonna show up, some aren't,

00:03:16.520 --> 00:03:18.100
you're gonna have access to more people

00:03:18.100 --> 00:03:21.320
than you ever did in some
ways 'cause the technology.

00:03:21.320 --> 00:03:23.660
It's all going to be maybe a mixed bag.

00:03:23.660 --> 00:03:26.760
And if it is, don't we
have to make it the best

00:03:26.760 --> 00:03:30.920
and most compelling television
in honor of the audience.

00:03:30.920 --> 00:03:33.110
- Yeah. I, 1000% agree with that.

00:03:33.110 --> 00:03:34.883
And I think, that's why
I say you really have,

00:03:34.883 --> 00:03:36.730
you literally have changed my life.

00:03:36.730 --> 00:03:37.580
And I've had people say that.

00:03:37.580 --> 00:03:41.063
I said, listen, "After Drew,
I'm not going nowhere."

00:03:41.063 --> 00:03:43.740
(audience laughing)
And so the truth is,

00:03:43.740 --> 00:03:46.030
you're able to have better access.

00:03:46.030 --> 00:03:49.970
And I think there will
certainly be times in both

00:03:49.970 --> 00:03:54.970
of our lives and in our
careers when being directly

00:03:55.670 --> 00:03:59.180
in front of the person makes a world

00:03:59.180 --> 00:04:01.960
of difference and may maybe necessary.

00:04:01.960 --> 00:04:05.210
But I think that in terms of having access

00:04:05.210 --> 00:04:10.210
to anybody in the world at
any time, what you all did

00:04:10.730 --> 00:04:15.560
in that first show lit, the
path for changing it forever.

00:04:15.560 --> 00:04:17.750
And I'm sure we would have
done it today, but I, you know

00:04:17.750 --> 00:04:19.240
I'm where there's no studio.

00:04:19.240 --> 00:04:21.140
So I'm like in the middle
of nowhere out here.

00:04:21.140 --> 00:04:22.880
You need a studio to be able to do it.

00:04:22.880 --> 00:04:24.490
- You really do.
- So if there was a studio

00:04:24.490 --> 00:04:26.700
within 500 miles I would have done it.

00:04:26.700 --> 00:04:27.700
- I'm so glad.

00:04:27.700 --> 00:04:30.610
And may we do it again in the future.

00:04:30.610 --> 00:04:34.970
Because I was so glad to
see you embracing this,

00:04:34.970 --> 00:04:37.680
you acknowledging us and us
moving forward in this way,

00:04:37.680 --> 00:04:40.120
because it does become a new normal.

00:04:40.120 --> 00:04:45.120
And it, for the girl who
felt really, honestly, again

00:04:45.250 --> 00:04:47.650
this is the part I don't talk about,

00:04:47.650 --> 00:04:50.390
is when you're fighting
for things in business,

00:04:50.390 --> 00:04:54.280
it's not comfortable, especially
when they're not done.

00:04:54.280 --> 00:04:56.330
You feel like the messy one, you feel

00:04:56.330 --> 00:04:58.920
like the one who might be
spending people's money

00:04:58.920 --> 00:05:00.590
in a improper way.

00:05:00.590 --> 00:05:02.760
You don't know if the
risk is gonna pay off

00:05:02.760 --> 00:05:05.670
but you get into your convictions
and you fight for them.

00:05:05.670 --> 00:05:08.190
And so when I saw you
embracing the technology,

00:05:08.190 --> 00:05:10.950
it told that person who
hung up the phone that day.

00:05:10.950 --> 00:05:13.130
'Cause I remember saying,
"Listen, you guys,

00:05:13.130 --> 00:05:14.550
we have got to do this.

00:05:14.550 --> 00:05:18.060
We have got to find a new way
for our future of television.

00:05:18.060 --> 00:05:19.450
This is not funny.

00:05:19.450 --> 00:05:21.500
We are in a really unprecedented time

00:05:21.500 --> 00:05:24.460
and we can't go on with
the home show forever.

00:05:24.460 --> 00:05:25.660
We just can't."

00:05:25.660 --> 00:05:29.260
- You did the thing that I
had to do a thousand times

00:05:29.260 --> 00:05:30.093
over the years.

00:05:30.093 --> 00:05:31.443
And when I first started,

00:05:32.990 --> 00:05:37.150
so let me applaud you for doing
the thing that every person,

00:05:37.150 --> 00:05:39.120
particularly every woman,

00:05:39.120 --> 00:05:42.020
needs to learn as soon as possible.

00:05:42.020 --> 00:05:47.020
Your gut is right for you.

00:05:47.960 --> 00:05:52.147
So when you feel something
in your gut and you think,

00:05:52.147 --> 00:05:55.650
"Wow, I need to, I need
to really move on that,"

00:05:55.650 --> 00:05:58.740
that means it is right for you.

00:05:58.740 --> 00:06:01.640
It doesn't mean that it is
right for everybody else.

00:06:01.640 --> 00:06:04.630
And I think it takes
women in particularly,

00:06:04.630 --> 00:06:09.280
a long long time to be
affirmed in their own beliefs.

00:06:09.280 --> 00:06:12.500
And that is the reason
actually we were the number

00:06:12.500 --> 00:06:16.060
one talk show for 25 years,

00:06:16.060 --> 00:06:21.060
is because it always came
back to, what do I feel?

00:06:21.980 --> 00:06:25.790
What do I feel is my real
intention in doing this?

00:06:25.790 --> 00:06:28.890
And I cannot do it if it's not true to me.

00:06:28.890 --> 00:06:31.280
Even if everybody else says,
and many times, you know, Drew,

00:06:31.280 --> 00:06:32.710
producers would come in with ideas,

00:06:32.710 --> 00:06:35.100
and I would say, "I can't do that.

00:06:35.100 --> 00:06:37.860
I don't know how I can sit in that chair

00:06:37.860 --> 00:06:40.441
and keep a straight face and do that.

00:06:40.441 --> 00:06:42.460
(audience and Drew laughing)

00:06:42.460 --> 00:06:43.710
I'm not gonna be able to do that."

00:06:43.710 --> 00:06:48.120
So I applaud you for going with your gut.

00:06:48.120 --> 00:06:51.430
'Cause your gut is always right for you.

00:06:51.430 --> 00:06:56.120
- Well, I think you've given
so much courage to everyone,

00:06:56.120 --> 00:07:00.950
especially women, to fight
for what we believe in.

00:07:00.950 --> 00:07:05.230
And I was gonna ask you
if you had any advice.

00:07:05.230 --> 00:07:08.330
May I ask when you
ended your first season,

00:07:08.330 --> 00:07:12.150
and you knew you were rounding
the corner in to season two,

00:07:12.150 --> 00:07:14.890
what were you telling yourself?

00:07:14.890 --> 00:07:17.183
- I actually was telling myself,

00:07:18.297 --> 00:07:20.930
"Self, you was right."

00:07:20.930 --> 00:07:24.730
Because as, most people
don't know this now,

00:07:24.730 --> 00:07:28.780
but when we launched in 1986,

00:07:30.510 --> 00:07:35.510
I had been a local talk show
in Chicago for two years.

00:07:36.450 --> 00:07:41.450
What I understood was that
there's a common nality,

00:07:41.460 --> 00:07:44.570
in our human experience and
that people are no different

00:07:44.570 --> 00:07:47.210
in Chicago than they are in New York,

00:07:47.210 --> 00:07:50.400
than they are in Idaho than
they are in Rhode Island,

00:07:50.400 --> 00:07:52.160
when it comes to the core.

00:07:52.160 --> 00:07:54.780
Now we're in, you know,
everybody's so divided

00:07:54.780 --> 00:07:57.430
about their politics and
their beliefs and so forth,

00:07:57.430 --> 00:07:59.680
but I still do believe at the core.

00:07:59.680 --> 00:08:01.070
We're all the same.

00:08:01.070 --> 00:08:04.490
If you ask blue States,
red States, purple States,

00:08:04.490 --> 00:08:06.200
what really matters to you?

00:08:06.200 --> 00:08:07.970
The answer is the same.

00:08:07.970 --> 00:08:09.510
People care about their families.

00:08:09.510 --> 00:08:12.060
They care about being
able to live decent lives.

00:08:12.060 --> 00:08:14.810
They care about being able
to do that in comfort,

00:08:14.810 --> 00:08:17.330
and do that with a sense of peace.

00:08:17.330 --> 00:08:22.330
And I still think in spite
of all the craziness,

00:08:22.920 --> 00:08:24.300
in spite of all the divisions,

00:08:24.300 --> 00:08:27.290
which is what everybody
talks about all the time

00:08:27.290 --> 00:08:30.580
is that we are more alike
than we are different,

00:08:30.580 --> 00:08:33.050
which is a very famous Maya Angelou line.

00:08:33.050 --> 00:08:34.600
She used to say that all the time.

00:08:34.600 --> 00:08:37.070
And in our humanness, we are,

00:08:37.070 --> 00:08:39.360
we all are just seeking the same things.

00:08:39.360 --> 00:08:42.500
And that's what this platform,
the Drew Barrymore Show,

00:08:42.500 --> 00:08:45.690
offers every day, is a
chance for those of us

00:08:45.690 --> 00:08:48.040
of like-mindedness.

00:08:48.040 --> 00:08:50.360
Those of us who are seeking the joy,

00:08:50.360 --> 00:08:52.604
who wants to wear some bows,

00:08:52.604 --> 00:08:54.310
(audience and Oprah laughing)

00:08:54.310 --> 00:08:56.290
- I went extra jaunty for you.

00:08:56.290 --> 00:08:59.743
I was like, bows and
flowers, it's Oprah, like,

00:08:59.743 --> 00:09:03.290
I'm surprised I don't have
bells on and like a bow.

00:09:03.290 --> 00:09:05.957
(upbeat music)

